# Claude Code — Project Power Guide

Everything you can configure **per-project** in Claude Code: what each file does, where it lives, when it loads, and a working example. Based on the official `.claude` directory reference.

---

## 0. The Big Picture

Claude Code reads config from two places every session:

| Root | Applies to | Commit to git? |
|---|---|---|
| `your-project/` (repo root + `.claude/`) | This project only | Yes (mostly) |
| `~/.claude/` (home dir) | Every project you work in | No — personal |

Project-level settings generally **override** global ones for scalar values (like `model`), and **combine** with global ones for list values (like `permissions.allow`).

If you set `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, all `~/.claude` paths move under that dir instead.

**Rule of thumb:** most people only ever touch `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/settings.json`. Everything else below is optional — add it when you actually need it.

---

## 1. `CLAUDE.md` — Project Instructions

**Location:** repo root (or `.claude/CLAUDE.md` if you want the root clean)
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** into context at the start of every session

The single most important file. This is where you tell Claude how your project works: conventions, commands, architecture, gotchas.

```markdown
# Project conventions

## Commands
- Build: `npm run build`
- Test: `npm test`
- Lint: `npm run lint`

## Stack
- TypeScript with strict mode
- React 19, functional components only

## Rules
- Named exports, never default exports
- Tests live next to source: `foo.ts` -> `foo.test.ts`
- All API routes return `{ data, error }` shape
```

**Tips:**
- Keep it under ~200 lines. It loads in full every session — bloat wastes context and can reduce how well Claude follows it.
- If something only matters for specific tasks (not every session), move it into a **skill** or a path-scoped **rule** instead (see below).
- List the commands you run most (build/test/format) so Claude doesn't have to guess or ask.
- Run `/memory` inside a session to open and edit `CLAUDE.md` directly.
- There's also `CLAUDE.local.md` (project root, **not** built-in — you create it and gitignore it yourself) for your own private preferences on this project, loaded alongside `CLAUDE.md`.

---

## 2. `.claude/settings.json` — Permissions, Hooks, Config

**Location:** `.claude/settings.json`
**Committed:** yes (shared with your team)
**Loads:** every session; overrides global `~/.claude/settings.json`; overridden by `settings.local.json`, CLI flags, and org-managed settings

Unlike `CLAUDE.md` (advisory — Claude *reads* it), this file is **enforced** whether Claude wants to follow it or not.

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": [
      "Bash(npm test *)",
      "Bash(npm run *)"
    ],
    "deny": [
      "Bash(rm -rf *)"
    ]
  },
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [{
      "matcher": "Edit|Write",
      "hooks": [{
        "type": "command",
        "command": "jq -r '.tool_input.file_path' | xargs npx prettier --write"
      }]
    }]
  }
}
```

What you can control here:

| Key | Does |
|---|---|
| `permissions` | Allow, deny, or prompt-before-use for specific tools/commands. Bash patterns support wildcards: `Bash(npm test *)` matches anything starting with `npm test`. |
| `hooks` | Run your own scripts at lifecycle events (before/after tool calls, session start/end, etc.) — **guaranteed** to run, unlike CLAUDE.md instructions. |
| `statusLine` | Customize the status line shown while Claude works. |
| `model` | Pin a default model for this project. |
| `env` | Environment variables set in every session. |
| `outputStyle` | Select a custom system-prompt style from `output-styles/`. |

**Note:** array settings (like `permissions.allow`) merge across all scopes (global + project + local). Scalar settings (like `model`) use the most specific scope that sets them.

### `.claude/settings.local.json` — Your Personal Overrides

**Committed:** no — gitignored automatically the first time Claude Code writes to it
**Loads:** highest priority of the user-editable settings files

Same schema as `settings.json`. Use it for permissions or defaults *you* want that shouldn't be forced on the whole team.

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(docker *)"]
  }
}
```

---

## 3. `.claude/rules/` — Topic-Scoped, Path-Gated Instructions

**Location:** `.claude/rules/*.md` (subfolders work too)
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** rules *without* `paths:` frontmatter load at session start (just like CLAUDE.md); rules *with* `paths:` load only when a matching file enters context

This is how you split a bloated `CLAUDE.md` into focused pieces that only load when relevant.

```markdown
---
paths:
  - "**/*.test.ts"
  - "**/*.test.tsx"
---

# Testing Rules

- Use descriptive test names: "should [expected] when [condition]"
- Mock external dependencies, not internal modules
- Clean up side effects in afterEach
```

```markdown
---
paths:
  - "src/api/**/*.ts"
---

# API Design Rules

- All endpoints must validate input with Zod schemas
- Return shape: { data: T } | { error: string }
- Rate limit all public endpoints
```

**Tip:** when `CLAUDE.md` starts creeping past ~200 lines, this is where the overflow should go. Like CLAUDE.md, rules are guidance — for something *enforced*, use hooks/permissions instead.

---

## 4. `.claude/skills/` — Reusable, Invokable Capabilities (the modern replacement for commands)

**Location:** `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (+ any supporting files in that folder)
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** invoked with `/name`, or auto-invoked by Claude when it matches your task to the skill's description

Skills are the current, recommended mechanism — they subsume the older "commands" system (see §5). A skill is a **folder**, not just a file, so you can bundle reference docs, checklists, scripts, or templates alongside the entrypoint.

```
.claude/skills/security-review/
├── SKILL.md
└── checklist.md
```

`SKILL.md`:
```markdown
---
description: Reviews code changes for security vulnerabilities, authentication gaps, and injection risks
disable-model-invocation: true
argument-hint: <branch-or-path>
---

## Diff to review

!`git diff $ARGUMENTS`

Audit the changes above for:

1. Injection vulnerabilities (SQL, XSS, command)
2. Authentication and authorization gaps
3. Hardcoded secrets or credentials

Use checklist.md in this skill directory for the full review checklist.

Report findings with severity ratings and remediation steps.
```

`checklist.md` (a supporting file Claude reads on demand):
```markdown
# Security Review Checklist

## Input Validation
- [ ] All user input sanitized before DB queries
- [ ] File upload MIME types validated
- [ ] Path traversal prevented on file operations

## Authentication
- [ ] JWT tokens expire after 24 hours
- [ ] API keys stored in environment variables
- [ ] Passwords hashed with bcrypt or argon2
```

### Key frontmatter fields

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `name` | Required. Becomes the slash command (`name: deploy` → `/deploy`). Falls back to folder name if omitted — set it explicitly. |
| `description` | Required. **The primary trigger** — Claude reads this to decide when to auto-load the skill. Write it to match real user intent, not a marketing summary. |
| `disable-model-invocation: true` | Only a human can trigger it via `/name` — Claude will never auto-invoke it. Good for destructive/manual-only actions like `/deploy`. |
| `user-invocable: false` | Opposite — hides it from the `/` menu; only Claude can invoke it automatically. |
| `allowed-tools` | Restrict which tools the skill can use while active (e.g. read-only skills). |
| `argument-hint` | Shown in autocomplete as a hint for what to type after the command. |
| `model` | Pin a specific model for this skill. |
| `context: fork` | Run as an isolated subagent with its own context window. |

### Other superpowers

- **Arguments:** `/deploy staging` → `$ARGUMENTS` = `"staging"`. Positional access via `$0`, `$1`, etc.
- **Live shell injection:** a line starting with `!` followed by a backtick command runs it and injects the output into the prompt (needs `Bash` permission), e.g. `` !`git diff --name-only HEAD~1` ``.
- **Bundled files:** Claude knows the skill's directory path automatically, so mentioning `checklist.md` inside the SKILL.md body lets Claude read it. In shell injection commands use `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` to reference the skill's own folder.
- If a skill and a command share a name, **the skill wins**.

---

## 5. `.claude/commands/` — Legacy Single-File Commands

**Location:** `.claude/commands/*.md`
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** invoked with `/name`

> **Custom commands have been merged into skills.** A file at `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and a skill at `.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md` both create `/deploy` and behave the same way. Existing `.claude/commands/` files keep working — but for **new** workflows, use `.claude/skills/` instead, since it gives you the same `/name` invocation plus a whole folder for supporting files.

```markdown
---
argument-hint: <issue-number>
---

!`gh issue view $ARGUMENTS`

Investigate and fix the issue above.

1. Trace the bug to its root cause
2. Implement the fix
3. Write or update tests
4. Summarize what you changed and why
```

---

## 6. `.claude/agents/` — Subagents

**Location:** `.claude/agents/*.md`
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** Claude spawns it automatically for matching tasks, or you `@`-mention it from autocomplete

Each file defines a specialized subagent: its own system prompt, its own tool access, optionally its own model — and critically, **its own fresh context window**, separate from your main conversation. Great for parallel work, isolated review tasks, or keeping noisy exploration out of your main thread.

```markdown
---
name: code-reviewer
description: Reviews code for correctness, security, and maintainability
tools: Read, Grep, Glob
---

You are a senior code reviewer. Review for:

1. Correctness: logic errors, edge cases, null handling
2. Security: injection, auth bypass, data exposure
3. Maintainability: naming, complexity, duplication

Every finding must include a concrete fix.
```

**Tips:**
- Restrict `tools:` per agent (e.g. read-only reviewers that can never edit).
- The `description` field is what lets Claude decide *when* to delegate to this subagent automatically.
- Type `@` in the chat and pick an agent from autocomplete to delegate directly, on demand.

### Persistent subagent memory: `.claude/agent-memory/`

Subagents can have their own memory, separate from your main session's auto memory. Only created if the agent's frontmatter sets `memory:`.

```yaml
memory: project   # -> .claude/agent-memory/<agent-name>/MEMORY.md (committed, shared with team)
memory: local     # -> .claude/agent-memory-local/ (gitignored, not shared)
memory: user      # -> ~/.claude/agent-memory/ (cross-project, personal)
```

The subagent writes and maintains its own `MEMORY.md` — you don't author it. It's loaded into the subagent's system prompt (first 200 lines / 25KB cap) each time it runs.

---

## 7. `.claude/workflows/` — Dynamic Multi-Agent Orchestration

**Location:** `.claude/workflows/*.js`
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** at startup; each file becomes a `/<name>` command

Each `.js` file is a script the runtime executes to spawn and coordinate *multiple* subagents in a structured pipeline. You don't typically hand-write these — Claude writes them, and you save a run from the `/workflows` UI with `s`.

A project workflow takes precedence over a personal one (`~/.claude/workflows/`) with the same name.

---

## 8. `.mcp.json` — Team-Shared External Tools (MCP servers)

**Location:** project **root** (not inside `.claude/`)
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** servers connect at session start; tool schemas load on demand via tool search

This is how you plug Claude Code into external tools/services (databases, APIs, browsers, project management tools, etc.) for the whole team.

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notion": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@notionhq/notion-mcp-server"],
      "env": {
        "NOTION_TOKEN": "${NOTION_TOKEN}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Tips:**
- Use `${VAR}` env references for secrets — never hardcode tokens in the committed file.
- For servers only *you* personally need (not the whole team), run `claude mcp add --scope user` instead — that writes to `~/.claude.json`, not `.mcp.json`.
- Claude Code loads MCP servers from **both** `.mcp.json` (project, shared) and `~/.claude.json` (personal) — project servers supplement, not replace, your personal ones.

---

## 9. `.worktreeinclude` — Files to Copy into New Git Worktrees

**Location:** project root
**Committed:** yes
**Loads:** read when Claude creates a git worktree (via `--worktree`, the `EnterWorktree` tool, or a subagent with `isolation: worktree`)

Worktrees are fresh checkouts — gitignored files like `.env` don't come along automatically. This file (using `.gitignore`-style patterns) lists which gitignored files *should* be copied into every new worktree.

```
# Local environment
.env
.env.local

# API credentials
config/secrets.json
```

Note: this is git-only. If you're on a different VCS, configure a `WorktreeCreate` hook and copy files inside the hook script instead.

---

## 10. `.claude/output-styles/` — Project-Shared Response Styles

**Location:** `.claude/output-styles/*.md`
**Committed:** yes (usually these are personal and live in `~/.claude/output-styles/` instead — put one here only if your whole team shares it, e.g. a "review mode" everyone uses)

Each file defines a chunk appended to the system prompt — by default this also **drops** the built-in software-engineering task instructions, so it's meant for adapting Claude Code beyond normal coding work (teaching mode, review mode, etc.).

```markdown
---
description: Explains reasoning and asks you to implement small pieces
keep-coding-instructions: true
---

After completing each task, add a brief "Why this approach" note
explaining the key design decision.

When a change is under 10 lines, ask the user to implement it
themselves by leaving a TODO(human) marker instead of writing it.
```

Set `keep-coding-instructions: true` if you want your additions layered *on top of* the defaults rather than replacing them. Select the style via `/config` or the `outputStyle` key in settings — changes take effect next session (system prompt is fixed at startup for caching).

---

## 11. Auto Memory — Claude's Own Notes (mostly global, worth knowing)

**Location:** `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` (global, keyed by repo path — not something you author in the repo itself)
**Loads:** `MEMORY.md` (first 200 lines / 25KB) loaded at session start; topic files read on demand

On by default. As Claude works in your project, it accumulates its own notes — build quirks, architecture observations, debugging insights — without you writing anything. It's plain markdown, so you can read, edit, or delete it anytime. Toggle with `/memory` or the `autoMemoryEnabled` setting.

---

## 12. Precedence — What Wins When Things Conflict

From lowest to highest priority for `settings.json`-style config:

1. Global `~/.claude/settings.json`
2. Project `.claude/settings.json`
3. Project `.claude/settings.local.json`
4. CLI flags (`--permission-mode`, `--settings`, etc.)
5. Org-managed `managed-settings.json` (always wins, can't be overridden)

For `CLAUDE.md`: global and project files are **both** loaded into context together (not merged key-by-key) — when instructions actually conflict, project-level wins.

Some environment variables override their equivalent setting too — check the env-vars reference if something isn't behaving as expected.

---

## 13. Quick Reference — "I want to..." → "Edit this"

| You want to… | Edit | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| Give Claude project context & conventions | `CLAUDE.md` | project or global |
| Allow/block specific tool calls | `settings.json` → `permissions` | project or global |
| Run a script before/after tool calls | `settings.json` → `hooks` | project or global |
| Set env vars for every session | `settings.json` → `env` | project or global |
| Keep personal overrides out of git | `settings.local.json` | project only |
| Add a `/name` prompt or capability | `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | project or global |
| Define a specialized subagent | `agents/*.md` | project or global |
| Orchestrate multiple subagents | `workflows/*.js` | project or global |
| Connect external tools (MCP) | `.mcp.json` | project only |
| Change how Claude formats responses | `output-styles/*.md` | project or global |
| Split CLAUDE.md by topic/path | `rules/*.md` | project or global |
| Copy secrets into new worktrees | `.worktreeinclude` | project only |

---

## 14. Example: A Fully Powered-Up Project

```
your-project/
├── CLAUDE.md                          # conventions, commands, stack
├── CLAUDE.local.md                    # (gitignored) your own personal notes
├── .mcp.json                          # shared MCP servers (e.g. GitHub, Notion)
├── .worktreeinclude                   # .env, secrets copied into new worktrees
└── .claude/
    ├── settings.json                  # permissions + hooks, committed
    ├── settings.local.json            # (gitignored) your personal overrides
    ├── rules/
    │   ├── testing.md                 # loads only for *.test.ts files
    │   └── api-design.md              # loads only for src/api/**
    ├── skills/
    │   ├── security-review/
    │   │   ├── SKILL.md               # /security-review <branch>
    │   │   └── checklist.md
    │   └── deploy/
    │       └── SKILL.md               # /deploy staging
    ├── agents/
    │   └── code-reviewer.md           # @code-reviewer, read-only tools
    ├── agent-memory/
    │   └── code-reviewer/MEMORY.md    # subagent's own persistent notes
    ├── workflows/
    │   └── release-pipeline.js        # /release-pipeline
    └── output-styles/
        └── teaching.md                # optional, team-shared style
```

---

## 15. Debugging Config

If a setting, hook, or file isn't taking effect:
- Run `/config` to inspect active settings.
- Check `settings precedence` — a higher-scope file or CLI flag may be silently overriding it.
- Confirm the file is in the right location (`.claude/` vs project root — several files, like `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json`, and `.worktreeinclude`, live at the project **root**, not inside `.claude/`).
- For skills/commands not appearing: confirm frontmatter is valid YAML and `name`/`description` are set.

---

*Compiled from Anthropic's official Claude Code documentation (code.claude.com/docs). Field names and behavior can change between releases — if something doesn't behave as documented here, check the current docs for that specific feature.*